<Project>
  <!--
    The sans faces every WEB host has to embed, and the one place that says so.

    The wasm Skia build ships exactly one face — Noto Mono — so a browser host without a real sans
    face renders every app monospaced, silently, with no error to search for. Both web hosts
    therefore embed the same two files, under the same logical names, and read them back at
    startup.

    They live here rather than inside either host because neither owns them. They were briefly in
    CupriFace.Web.Mono/Assets, which made the NativeAOT-LLVM host reach into the Mono host's package
    folder for a shared asset — backwards, and the kind of coupling that decides which host is
    "real" by accident.

    Importing this rather than copying the item group is deliberate: the two hosts have already
    drifted apart once per shared thing that was duplicated instead of shared (see the IME gap and
    the font-path break). One import, one guard, both hosts.
  -->
  <PropertyGroup>
    <CupriWebFontsDir>$(MSBuildThisFileDirectory)WebFonts\</CupriWebFontsDir>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <EmbeddedResource Include="$(CupriWebFontsDir)*.ttf" LogicalName="fonts.%(Filename)%(Extension)" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <!-- A glob that matches nothing is not an MSBuild error, so the build stays green and the app
       dies at first paint dereferencing a null resource stream instead. That is how moving these
       files broke one host's browser gate and nothing else. Fail here, where the cause is. -->
  <Target Name="AssertWebFontsEmbedded" BeforeTargets="CoreCompile">
    <Error Condition="'@(EmbeddedResource->WithMetadataValue('Extension', '.ttf'))' == ''"
           Text="No .ttf embedded: the sans faces a web host loads at startup were not found under $(CupriWebFontsDir). Without them every app renders monospaced, and the resource read at startup throws." />
  </Target>
</Project>
